WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.848 --> 00:00:01.448
Hello.

2
00:00:01.037 --> 00:00:06.010
I'm Greg Oltmanns, Chief Financial Officer at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.494
Laura asked me to explain my limit before
you start on the three proposals,

4
00:00:09.494 --> 00:00:10.866
so here it is.

5
00:00:10.866 --> 00:00:13.908
Forty-seven million dollars
over five years.

6
00:00:13.908 --> 00:00:16.245
That's the ceiling, and it's
for everything.

7
00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:19.364
The new EHR, any extra staff or
hardware we'd have to add,

8
00:00:19.364 --> 00:00:23.377
and any Extended Support we'd have to buy from
Plexmoor if the new system isn't

9
00:00:23.377 --> 00:00:23.983
live in time.

10
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:27.967
That Extended Support would be three point
three million dollars for 2029.

11
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:32.334
That's one hundred fifty percent of the two point
two million we pay Plexmoor today.

12
00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:34.153
Why forty-seven million?

13
00:00:34.153 --> 00:00:35.888
Let me give you the context.

14
00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:40.059
Our operating budget is about three hundred
seventy-five million dollars a year.

15
00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:43.275
Our operating margin, what's left
after we pay for everything,

16
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:45.161
is about one point two percent.

17
00:00:45.161 --> 00:00:48.424
That's roughly four and a half
million dollars a year.

18
00:00:48.424 --> 00:00:52.107
So a surprise of a few million dollars
isn't a rounding error for us.

19
00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:53.377
It can wipe out a whole year of margin.

20
00:00:53.377 --> 00:00:57.994
And a year with no margin means no new
equipment, no raises, no cushion.

21
00:00:57.994 --> 00:00:59.903
Here's something I've learned in this job.

22
00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:01.834
A vendor's quote is written to win.

23
00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:03.799
It's accurate.

24
00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:04.633
Every number in it is true.

25
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:09.009
But a quote is what the vendor
wants you to compare,

26
00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:11.704
and my job is to know what we
will actually pay.

27
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.539
Those are not always the same number.

28
00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:16.181
I also care about what
happens after we sign.

29
00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:16.781
Can the price go up?

30
00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:19.084
Can we get out if it goes badly?

31
00:01:19.084 --> 00:01:23.095
If we leave, can we take our own data with
us, and what does that cost?

32
00:01:23.095 --> 00:01:25.927
A contract we can't get out
of is a cost too.

33
00:01:25.927 --> 00:01:27.714
It just doesn't show up in year one.

34
00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:31.086
Now, Laura's told you that Doug and I
each have a favorite vendor,

35
00:01:31.086 --> 00:01:32.804
and we don't agree.

36
00:01:32.804 --> 00:01:33.404
That's true.

37
00:01:32.804 --> 00:01:34.315
I'm not going to tell you mine.

38
00:01:34.315 --> 00:01:36.390
I want your numbers and your reasoning,

39
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.212
not my opinion coming back to me
with a new cover page.

40
00:01:39.212 --> 00:01:43.059
Show me the full five-year cost for each
vendor, show me how you got it,

41
00:01:43.059 --> 00:01:45.407
and tell me which contract
terms worry you.

42
00:01:45.407 --> 00:01:49.427
If a number is your estimate and not a fact
from the proposal, label it.

43
00:01:49.427 --> 00:01:53.280
I'd rather see an honest estimate
than a confident guess.

